    <edge id="e1626" source="mlsys.multiple-comparisons" target="mlsys.selection-bias"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">limits false declarations of a winner that feed</data><data key="conditions">When many candidates, metrics, or slices are compared on the same evaluation data; it does not correct the winner's overstated effect size.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1627" source="mlsys.online-experiment" target="mlsys.multiple-comparisons"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">controls error rates across many decision metrics with</data><data key="conditions">When an experiment's launch decision rests on several metrics or segments.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1628" source="mlsys.model-assisted-labeling" target="mlsys.labels"><data key="relation">trades-off</data><data key="statement">trades annotation throughput against the quality of</data><data key="conditions">When annotators review model drafts rather than labeling from scratch, so anchoring can carry model errors into labels.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1629" source="mlsys.model-assisted-labeling" target="mlsys.weak-supervision"><data key="relation">alternative-to</data><data key="statement">keeps a per-item human review step that is not part of</data><data key="conditions">When the label budget allows human confirmation of each draft label.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1630" source="mlsys.forward-error-correction" target="mlsys.scale-up-interconnect"><data key="relation">part-of</data><data key="statement">corrects bit errors on the links of</data><data key="conditions">When high-speed SerDes links run a FEC code on every hop, adding decoding delay to each.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1631" source="mlsys.rdma" target="mlsys.forward-error-correction"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">avoids costly go-back-N retransmissions on fast links through</data><data key="conditions">When the RDMA transport recovers from loss by go-back-N retransmission, so even rare uncorrected bit errors would stall throughput.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1632" source="mlsys.object-storage" target="mlsys.erasure-coding"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">protects stored objects with</data><data key="conditions">When the store keeps parity fragments rather than full copies.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1633" source="mlsys.erasure-coding" target="mlsys.replication-failover"><data key="relation">alternative-to</data><data key="statement">provides storage durability with less overhead than the full-copy replication in</data><data key="conditions">For durable stored data at comparable failure tolerance, not for request failover; reads after a loss pay reconstruction cost.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1634" source="mlsys.bandwidth-delay-product" target="mlsys.congestion-control"><data key="relation">determines</data><data key="statement">sets the window that</data><data key="conditions">When a window-controlled flow must keep a long or fast path full while acknowledgments are in flight.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1635" source="mlsys.network-propagation-delay" target="mlsys.bandwidth-delay-product"><data key="relation">determines</data><data key="statement">sets the round-trip floor of</data><data key="conditions">For a single path; queueing and processing delay add to the round-trip time.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1636" source="mlsys.backfill-scheduling" target="mlsys.utilization"><data key="relation">increases</data><data key="statement">fills idle resources to raise</data><data key="conditions">When job runtime estimates are accurate enough that backfilled jobs finish before the reserved start.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1637" source="mlsys.backfill-scheduling" target="mlsys.queueing"><data key="relation">reduces</data><data key="statement">shortens the wait of small jobs in</data><data key="conditions">When small jobs fit into gaps ahead of a large gang job's reserved start and their runtime estimates are accurate.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1638" source="mlsys.traffic-classes" target="mlsys.performance-interference"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">isolates competing flows to limit</data><data key="conditions">When collective, storage, and control traffic share the same links and switches and are mapped to separate classes.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1639" source="mlsys.lossless-fabric" target="mlsys.traffic-classes"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">applies pause-based flow control per priority class through</data><data key="conditions">When the fabric is made lossless with priority flow control, as in RoCE, rather than with credit-based link flow control.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1640" source="mlsys.telemetry-sampling" target="mlsys.observability"><data key="relation">trades-off</data><data key="statement">trades telemetry cost against the investigative reach of</data><data key="conditions">When sampling drops the records, or aggregation drops the label detail, that would explain a rare failure.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1641" source="mlsys.distributed-tracing" target="mlsys.telemetry-sampling"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">keeps a fraction of request traces through</data><data key="conditions">When recording every span for every request would exceed storage or processing budgets.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1642" source="mlsys.zero-copy-dma" target="mlsys.dma-cache-maintenance"><data key="relation">requires</data><data key="statement">stays correct on non-coherent hardware only with</data><data key="conditions">When the CPU accesses cached buffers that a sensor or accelerator also accesses by DMA without hardware coherence.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1643" source="mlsys.coherent-interconnect" target="mlsys.dma-cache-maintenance"><data key="relation">alternative-to</data><data key="statement">replaces in hardware the software work of</data><data key="conditions">When the link keeps device and host caches coherent, making explicit cache maintenance unnecessary.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1644" source="mlsys.expert-offloading" target="mlsys.cpu-gpu-hybrid-inference"><data key="relation">alternative-to</data><data key="statement">moves expert weights to the accelerator instead of computing on the host as in</data><data key="conditions">When mixture-of-experts weights exceed accelerator memory and must stay partly in host memory.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1645" source="mlsys.host-device-interconnect" target="mlsys.expert-offloading"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">bounds the expert-loading rate of</data><data key="conditions">When each batch needs experts that are not already resident in accelerator memory.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1646" source="mlsys.expert-offloading" target="mlsys.expert-routing"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">prefetches the experts predicted by</data><data key="conditions">When routing for upcoming layers or batches can be predicted early enough to hide the transfer.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1647" source="mlsys.variable-length-parallelism" target="mlsys.sequence-parallel"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">varies the degree and layout of</data><data key="conditions">When training sequence lengths vary widely within and across steps.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1648" source="mlsys.variable-length-parallelism" target="mlsys.stragglers"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">rebalances per-rank attention work to limit</data><data key="conditions">When a few long documents would otherwise make some ranks finish each step much later than others.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1649" source="mlsys.variable-length-parallelism" target="mlsys.sequence-packing"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">chooses document-to-rank assignments in</data><data key="conditions">When packed sequences are distributed across the ranks of a sequence-parallel group.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1650" source="mlsys.fine-grained-accelerator-sharing" target="mlsys.gpu-partitioning"><data key="relation">specializes</data><data key="statement">is the sub-kernel scheduling form of</data><data key="conditions">When co-located workloads need tighter latency isolation than whole-kernel time slicing gives and more flexibility than fixed hardware partitions.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1651" source="mlsys.fine-grained-accelerator-sharing" target="mlsys.performance-interference"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">bounds the slowdown of latency-sensitive work from</data><data key="conditions">When the scheduler can preempt or throttle best-effort kernels within a kernel's execution.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1652" source="mlsys.intra-device-overlap" target="mlsys.pd-disaggregation"><data key="relation">alternative-to</data><data key="statement">co-locates on one device the phases separated by</data><data key="conditions">When a deployment chooses between running prefill and decode on the same devices or on separate pools.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1653" source="mlsys.intra-device-overlap" target="mlsys.utilization"><data key="relation">increases</data><data key="statement">keeps several hardware resources busy at once, raising</data><data key="conditions">When the co-scheduled operations are bound by different resources and scheduling overhead stays small.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1654" source="mlsys.intra-device-overlap" target="mlsys.chunked-prefill"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">overlaps the prefill and decode work in the mixed batches formed by</data><data key="conditions">When chunked prefill puts prefill chunks and decode steps in the same iteration, and a fused kernel runs them on the same compute units.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1655" source="mlsys.graph-pipeline-parallelism" target="mlsys.pipeline-parallel"><data key="relation">specializes</data><data key="statement">is the branching-graph form of</data><data key="conditions">When the model's layer graph has branches with no data dependence between them.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1656" source="mlsys.graph-pipeline-parallelism" target="mlsys.pipeline-bubble"><data key="relation">reduces</data><data key="statement">shortens the pipeline's critical path, shrinking the fill and drain time of</data><data key="conditions">Relative to a linearized schedule on the same devices and microbatch count, when the branches are balanced closely enough that parallel stages finish at similar times.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1657" source="mlsys.rag-pipeline-scheduling" target="mlsys.ttft"><data key="relation">reduces</data><data key="statement">overlaps retrieval with generation work to shorten</data><data key="conditions">When retrieval sits on the request's critical path before prefill.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1658" source="mlsys.rag-pipeline-scheduling" target="mlsys.queueing"><data key="relation">reduces</data><data key="statement">avoids waiting for generation memory through joint configuration and scheduling, cutting</data><data key="conditions">When a fixed RAG configuration would wait for accelerator memory but another configuration meeting the query's quality target fits.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1659" source="mlsys.cross-stack-profiling" target="mlsys.profiling"><data key="relation">specializes</data><data key="statement">is the cross-layer form of</data><data key="conditions">When the profiler records events at more than one software layer and can link each device event to the host call that issued it.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1660" source="mlsys.cross-stack-profiling" target="mlsys.dispatch"><data key="relation">detects</data><data key="statement">exposes host-side gaps between device kernels caused by</data><data key="conditions">When host and device events share a clock or are aligned by correlation identifiers.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1661" source="mlsys.bottleneck-analysis" target="mlsys.cross-stack-profiling"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">locates the limiting software layer with</data><data key="conditions">When the bottleneck lies in host code or framework overhead rather than in a single kernel.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1662" source="mlsys.profiling" target="mlsys.memory-footprint"><data key="relation">measures</data><data key="statement">records per-tensor and per-phase allocations that make up</data><data key="conditions">When the profiler tracks allocations over a training step rather than only peak usage.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1663" source="mlsys.loss-scaling" target="mlsys.training-instability"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">keeps small gradients representable to limit</data><data key="conditions">When low-precision gradients would otherwise underflow to zero or overflow to infinity.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1664" source="mlsys.profiling" target="mlsys.stragglers"><data key="relation">detects</data><data key="statement">exposes the slow ranks behind</data><data key="conditions">When per-rank step and collective times are recorded and compared.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1665" source="mlsys.latency-percentiles" target="mlsys.ttft"><data key="relation">measures</data><data key="statement">summarizes the distribution of</data><data key="conditions">When time to first token is recorded per request rather than averaged.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1666" source="mlsys.autoscaling" target="mlsys.queueing"><data key="relation">reduces</data><data key="statement">adds capacity before requests wait, cutting</data><data key="conditions">When new replicas become ready faster than the load rises; cold starts delay the effect.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1667" source="mlsys.kv-cache-eviction" target="mlsys.kv-cache"><data key="relation">reduces</data><data key="statement">bounds the resident size of</data><data key="conditions">When the evicted entries receive little attention, so quality holds.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1668" source="mlsys.kv-cache-offload" target="mlsys.kv-cache"><data key="relation">reduces</data><data key="statement">moves cold blocks out of accelerator memory, shrinking the resident part of</data><data key="conditions">When transfer bandwidth to host memory or storage can bring blocks back before they are needed.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1669" source="mlsys.online-experiment" target="mlsys.selection-bias"><data key="relation">detects</data><data key="statement">tests on live traffic a winner chosen under</data><data key="conditions">When a model picked by offline comparison is exposed to a controlled share of real users.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1670" source="mlsys.duty-cycling" target="mlsys.thermal-dynamics"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">lowers average power to limit the heating behind</data><data key="conditions">When average power, not peak power, sets the device's sustained temperature.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1671" source="mlsys.cost-attribution" target="mlsys.cost-per-token"><data key="relation">measures</data><data key="statement">assigns infrastructure spend to requests to compute</data><data key="conditions">When usage is metered per request, model, or tenant.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1672" source="mlsys.quantization" target="mlsys.cost-per-token"><data key="relation">reduces</data><data key="statement">raises throughput per accelerator, cutting</data><data key="conditions">When the lower precision keeps quality acceptable and the serving stack has kernels for it.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1673" source="mlsys.sliced-evaluation" target="mlsys.over-refusal"><data key="relation">detects</data><data key="statement">exposes, on a slice of borderline benign prompts,</data><data key="conditions">When the evaluation set includes benign prompts that resemble harmful ones.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1674" source="mlsys.preference-optimization" target="mlsys.over-refusal"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">teaches helpful answers to borderline benign requests, limiting</data><data key="conditions">When the preference data includes benign requests that resemble harmful ones, paired with helpful responses.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1675" source="mlsys.admission" target="mlsys.kv-cache"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">caps the number of concurrent sequences whose state must stay resident in</data><data key="conditions">When admission is based on projected KV memory rather than request count alone.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1676" source="mlsys.energy-measurement" target="mlsys.energy-per-inference"><data key="relation">measures</data><data key="statement">records the energy behind</data><data key="conditions">When device power is sampled over the same window as the inference workload.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1677" source="mlsys.inter-token-latency" target="mlsys.tpot"><data key="relation">measures</data><data key="statement">gives the per-gap distribution whose request average is</data><data key="conditions">When streamed outputs are timestamped individually rather than only at request end.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1678" source="mlsys.model-swapping" target="mlsys.multi-model-serving"><data key="relation">part-of</data><data key="statement">is the weight-movement mechanism of</data><data key="conditions">When more models are offered than fit in accelerator memory at once.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1679" source="mlsys.host-device-interconnect" target="mlsys.model-swapping"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">bounds how fast models can be loaded in</data><data key="conditions">When a model's weights must move from host memory or storage before it can serve.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1680" source="mlsys.model-swapping" target="mlsys.utilization"><data key="relation">increases</data><data key="statement">lets otherwise idle accelerators serve other models, raising</data><data key="conditions">When demand across models is uneven over time and the transfer latency fits the service objective.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1681" source="mlsys.paged-kv" target="mlsys.kv-memory-allocation"><data key="relation">specializes</data><data key="statement">is the block-based form of</data><data key="conditions">When attention state is stored in fixed-size blocks mapped by a block table.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1682" source="mlsys.kv-memory-allocation" target="mlsys.memory-fragmentation"><data key="relation">determines</data><data key="statement">sets how much KV memory is lost to</data><data key="conditions">When sequences of varying and growing length share one accelerator memory pool.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1683" source="mlsys.kv-memory-allocation" target="mlsys.kv-cache"><data key="relation">implements</data><data key="statement">provides the storage that holds</data><data key="conditions">When a serving engine manages the cache's memory rather than the model framework.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1684" source="mlsys.feed-forward-block" target="mlsys.transformer"><data key="relation">part-of</data><data key="statement">is the position-wise sublayer paired with attention in each block of</data><data key="conditions">In standard Transformer blocks, where each block alternates an attention sublayer and a feed-forward sublayer under residual connections.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1685" source="mlsys.mixture-of-experts" target="mlsys.feed-forward-block"><data key="relation">alternative-to</data><data key="statement">replaces the dense sublayer with routed experts as an alternative to</data><data key="conditions">When a block's feed-forward sublayer is implemented as several routed experts instead of one dense network.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1686" source="mlsys.feed-forward-block" target="mlsys.matrix-multiply"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">executes its projections as</data><data key="conditions">The projections are dense GEMMs over all tokens, so in dense Transformers they dominate parameter count and prefill compute; gated variants add a third projection.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1687" source="mlsys.activation-sparsity" target="mlsys.feed-forward-block"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">skips the predicted-inactive neurons of</data><data key="conditions">When a hidden unit's activation is predicted near zero for the current token, so its weight rows need not be read or multiplied.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1688" source="mlsys.arrival-rate" target="mlsys.utilization"><data key="relation">increases</data><data key="statement">raises, as a fraction of service capacity, the</data><data key="conditions">When service capacity is fixed and the system is stable, so utilization is the ratio of arrival rate to service capacity.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1689" source="mlsys.dispatch" target="mlsys.bound-regime"><data key="relation">determines</data><data key="statement">places small operations on the overhead-bound side of</data><data key="conditions">When per-kernel work is so small that host launch and orchestration time exceeds device execution time, as in small-batch decode of small models.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1690" source="mlsys.service-level-agreement" target="mlsys.sli"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">is adjudicated on an agreed</data><data key="conditions">When the contract names the indicator, its measurement window, and who measures it.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1691" source="mlsys.requirements" target="mlsys.service-level-agreement"><data key="relation">determines</data><data key="statement">set the externally promised terms of</data><data key="conditions">When service commitments to customers are part of the stated deployment requirements.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1692" source="mlsys.head-of-line-blocking" target="mlsys.tail-latency"><data key="relation">increases</data><data key="statement">lengthens, through long items ahead of short ones, the</data><data key="conditions">When service is first-come-first-served and service times vary widely, so one long request delays every shorter one behind it.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1693" source="mlsys.continuous-batching" target="mlsys.head-of-line-blocking"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">admits and retires requests each iteration to avoid</data><data key="conditions">When finished sequences would otherwise wait for the longest sequence in a static batch before new work is admitted.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1694" source="mlsys.chunked-prefill" target="mlsys.head-of-line-blocking"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">bounds prefill work per iteration to limit</data><data key="conditions">When a long prompt's prefill would otherwise hold the decode step of every co-batched request.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1695" source="mlsys.lossless-fabric" target="mlsys.head-of-line-blocking"><data key="relation">causes</data><data key="statement">can cause, through pause frames that stop a whole port or class,</data><data key="conditions">When priority flow control pauses all traffic in a class because one downstream queue is congested.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1696" source="mlsys.open-closed-loop-load" target="mlsys.arrival-rate"><data key="relation">determines</data><data key="statement">sets whether responses throttle the</data><data key="conditions">Closed-loop clients cap concurrency at their own count, so arrival rate falls as latency rises; open-loop arrivals do not respond to latency.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1697" source="mlsys.open-closed-loop-load" target="mlsys.latency-throughput-curve"><data key="relation">determines</data><data key="statement">changes, through response feedback and concurrency limits, the offered load behind</data><data key="conditions">When generator concurrency, think time, and response feedback change offered arrivals, the measured curve must be interpreted under that load model; closed-loop tests can still reach saturation.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1698" source="mlsys.inference-load-scenario" target="mlsys.open-closed-loop-load"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">fixes as open or closed loop the</data><data key="conditions">Server scenarios issue queries by a timed random process; single-stream and multi-stream scenarios wait for each response.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1699" source="mlsys.transfer-learning" target="mlsys.time-to-train"><data key="relation">reduces</data><data key="statement">shortens the target-task</data><data key="conditions">When a suitable pretrained model exists and fine-tuning converges in far fewer steps than training from scratch.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1700" source="mlsys.pretraining" target="mlsys.compute-optimal-allocation"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">chooses model size and token count with</data><data key="conditions">When a fixed training compute budget is allocated before the run and the fitted law covers the target scale.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1701" source="mlsys.diffusion-feature-caching" target="mlsys.diffusion-model"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">reuses intermediate features across the denoising steps of</data><data key="conditions">When adjacent sampling steps produce similar features in some blocks, so those blocks can be skipped and their cached output reused.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1702" source="mlsys.experiment-budget" target="mlsys.scaling-laws"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">allocates small proxy runs to fit, and a large run to exploit,</data><data key="conditions">When the budget is split between runs that fit a law at small scale and the final run whose size the law predicts; extrapolation beyond the fitted range is not guaranteed.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1703" source="mlsys.inference-compute-estimate" target="mlsys.memory-footprint"><data key="relation">measures</data><data key="statement">estimates the parameter and KV portions of</data><data key="conditions">When parameter bytes follow from count and precision and KV bytes from layers, KV heads, head dimension, context, batch, and precision; runtime buffers and activations are excluded.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1704" source="mlsys.dispatch" target="mlsys.serial-fraction"><data key="relation">increases</data><data key="statement">contributes host-side, non-overlapped work to</data><data key="conditions">When launch and orchestration time is not overlapped with device execution, so it stays constant as device throughput or worker count grows.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1705" source="mlsys.all-reduce" target="mlsys.collectives"><data key="relation">specializes</data><data key="statement">is a kind of</data><data key="conditions">When every participant contributes an equal-shaped tensor and every participant receives the full reduced result.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1706" source="mlsys.ring-allreduce" target="mlsys.all-reduce"><data key="relation">implements</data><data key="statement">is an algorithm for</data><data key="conditions">When participants can be arranged in a ring with comparable link bandwidth.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1707" source="mlsys.tree-allreduce" target="mlsys.all-reduce"><data key="relation">implements</data><data key="statement">is an algorithm for</data><data key="conditions">When participants are arranged in trees or hierarchical groups so latency grows logarithmically.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1708" source="mlsys.in-network-reduction" target="mlsys.all-reduce"><data key="relation">implements</data><data key="statement">performs inside switches the reduction steps of</data><data key="conditions">When switches support aggregation for the data type and reduction operation used and also distribute the reduced result to every participant.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1709" source="mlsys.data-parallel" target="mlsys.all-reduce"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">synchronizes gradients through</data><data key="conditions">When replicas combine gradients before each optimizer step, as in synchronous data parallelism.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1710" source="mlsys.reduce-scatter-all-gather" target="mlsys.collectives"><data key="relation">specializes</data><data key="statement">are kinds of</data><data key="conditions">When each participant holds or receives a distinct shard rather than the whole tensor.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1711" source="mlsys.state-sharding" target="mlsys.reduce-scatter-all-gather"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">all-gathers parameters before use and reduce-scatters gradients after, through</data><data key="conditions">When parameters, gradients, or optimizer state are sharded across data-parallel workers, as in ZeRO stage 3 or FSDP.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1712" source="mlsys.ring-allreduce" target="mlsys.reduce-scatter-all-gather"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">is composed of</data><data key="conditions">When all-reduce is decomposed into a reduce-scatter followed by an all-gather around the ring.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1713" source="mlsys.tensor-parallel" target="mlsys.reduce-scatter-all-gather"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">replaces per-layer all-reduce with</data><data key="conditions">When tensor-parallel layers are combined with Megatron-style sequence parallelism so each all-reduce becomes a reduce-scatter and all-gather pair.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1714" source="mlsys.torus-topology" target="mlsys.network-topology"><data key="relation">specializes</data><data key="statement">is a kind of</data><data key="conditions">When endpoints connect to neighbors directly, without a switch hierarchy.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1715" source="mlsys.torus-topology" target="mlsys.fat-tree"><data key="relation">alternative-to</data><data key="statement">is an alternative to</data><data key="conditions">Both connect accelerator nodes at scale; a torus saves switch cost and suits ring collectives, while a non-oversubscribed Clos fat-tree with suitable routing gives uniform bisection bandwidth for arbitrary traffic.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1716" source="mlsys.torus-topology" target="mlsys.optical-circuit-switching"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">can be reconfigured and routed around failed nodes by</data><data key="conditions">When torus links pass through an optical circuit switch so slices of a pod can be joined into different torus shapes, as in TPU pods.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1717" source="mlsys.ring-allreduce" target="mlsys.torus-topology"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">runs at full link bandwidth on the rings of</data><data key="conditions">When each ring of the collective maps onto one torus dimension so every step uses a direct link.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1718" source="mlsys.torus-topology" target="mlsys.all-to-all"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">bounds, through multi-hop routing and lower bisection bandwidth,</data><data key="conditions">When exchange traffic crosses many torus hops rather than staying among neighbors.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1719" source="mlsys.network-oversubscription" target="mlsys.bisection-bandwidth"><data key="relation">reduces</data><data key="statement">lowers, below the sum of endpoint links,</data><data key="conditions">When traffic crosses the oversubscribed tier rather than staying under one leaf switch.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1720" source="mlsys.network-oversubscription" target="mlsys.collectives"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">caps the achievable rate of cross-tier</data><data key="conditions">When synchronized collective flows from many endpoints exceed a leaf switch's uplink capacity at the same moment.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1721" source="mlsys.network-oversubscription" target="mlsys.tco"><data key="relation">reduces</data><data key="statement">lowers switch and cabling cost within</data><data key="conditions">When fewer uplinks and spine switches are provisioned per endpoint and the workload tolerates the contention.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1722" source="mlsys.fusion" target="mlsys.arithmetic-intensity"><data key="relation">increases</data><data key="statement">raises, by keeping intermediates on chip instead of round-tripping them through device memory,</data><data key="conditions">When the fused operations are memory-bound elementwise or reduction kernels whose intermediates would otherwise be written to and read back from device memory.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1723" source="mlsys.quantization" target="mlsys.memory-footprint"><data key="relation">reduces</data><data key="statement">shrinks the bytes of stored weights, activations, or caches in</data><data key="conditions">When tensors are stored in the reduced format rather than only computed in it; dequantization buffers and scale factors add back a small share.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1724" source="mlsys.host-memory" target="mlsys.memory-hierarchy"><data key="relation">part-of</data><data key="statement">is the capacity tier above device memory in</data><data key="conditions">When the accelerator is attached to a host that has its own DRAM.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1725" source="mlsys.memory-offloading" target="mlsys.host-memory"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">spills optimizer state, parameters, or activations into</data><data key="conditions">When per-device memory cannot hold the full training state.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1726" source="mlsys.kv-cache-offload" target="mlsys.host-memory"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">keeps evicted KV blocks in</data><data key="conditions">When blocks are likely to be reused and host memory is cheaper than device memory.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1727" source="mlsys.host-data-loader" target="mlsys.host-memory"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">stages decoded batches in page-locked regions of</data><data key="conditions">When batches are copied to the device by DMA from pinned host buffers.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1728" source="mlsys.schedule-separation" target="mlsys.tiling"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">names, as a schedule primitive,</data><data key="conditions">When tile sizes and loop order are schedule choices rather than part of the algorithm's definition.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1729" source="mlsys.schedule-separation" target="mlsys.autotuning"><data key="relation">enables</data><data key="statement">exposes the search space explored by</data><data key="conditions">When schedule parameters are enumerable and the algorithm is fixed, so candidate implementations differ only in schedule.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1730" source="mlsys.schedule-separation" target="mlsys.intermediate-representation"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">applies schedule transformations to the loop-level</data><data key="conditions">When the compiler lowers the algorithm to a loop nest that schedule primitives rewrite.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1731" source="mlsys.schedule-separation" target="mlsys.software-pipelining"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">selects, as a schedule primitive,</data><data key="conditions">When pipelining stages are chosen by the schedule rather than written by hand in the kernel.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1732" source="mlsys.kernel-synchronization" target="mlsys.thread-hierarchy"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">coordinates threads at the scopes defined by</data><data key="conditions">When threads of a block or warp share on-chip memory and must order their accesses.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1733" source="mlsys.kernel-synchronization" target="mlsys.numerical-nondeterminism"><data key="relation">causes</data><data key="statement">introduces order-dependent floating-point results through contended</data><data key="conditions">When partial sums are combined with atomic adds whose completion order varies from run to run.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1734" source="mlsys.kernel-synchronization" target="mlsys.compute-throughput"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">serializes contended updates, bounding achieved</data><data key="conditions">When many threads atomically update the same address, as in histogram, scatter-add, or gradient-aggregation kernels.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1735" source="mlsys.sparse-tensor-kernels" target="mlsys.kernel-synchronization"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">scatter-adds irregular contributions through</data><data key="conditions">When several threads contribute to the same output element.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1736" source="mlsys.link-reach" target="mlsys.scale-up-interconnect"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">bounds the physical extent of</data><data key="conditions">When scale-up links run over passive copper, so the domain must fit within copper reach, typically one or a few racks.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1737" source="mlsys.link-reach" target="mlsys.rack-power-density"><data key="relation">increases</data><data key="statement">forces accelerators into few racks, raising</data><data key="conditions">When a scale-up domain must stay within copper reach rather than spreading across the floor.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1738" source="mlsys.co-packaged-optics" target="mlsys.link-reach"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">extends beyond copper, at lower energy per bit than pluggable modules,</data><data key="conditions">When links must span more than copper reach allows.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1739" source="mlsys.storage-access-pattern" target="mlsys.dataset-storage-layout"><data key="relation">determines</data><data key="statement">motivates large sequential shards in</data><data key="conditions">When training streams whole corpora so sequential bandwidth, not IOPS, is the binding resource.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1740" source="mlsys.storage-access-pattern" target="mlsys.input-pipeline"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">bounds the read stage of</data><data key="conditions">When examples are fetched by many small random reads from media or stores with high per-request latency.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1741" source="mlsys.storage-access-pattern" target="mlsys.storage-tiering"><data key="relation">determines</data><data key="statement">decides which tier serves which data in</data><data key="conditions">When random-access working sets, such as retrieval indexes or hot shards, are kept on NVMe or in memory while bulk corpora stream from capacity tiers.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1742" source="mlsys.tensor-parallel" target="mlsys.hybrid-parallel"><data key="relation">part-of</data><data key="statement">is one dimension of</data><data key="conditions">When tensor-parallel groups are placed within the fastest scale-up domain and combined with data and pipeline parallelism across nodes.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1743" source="mlsys.data-parallel" target="mlsys.hybrid-parallel"><data key="relation">part-of</data><data key="statement">is the outermost dimension of</data><data key="conditions">When replicas of a tensor- or pipeline-parallel model group are themselves data-parallel across nodes.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1744" source="mlsys.matrix-unit" target="mlsys.compute-throughput"><data key="relation">increases</data><data key="statement">supplies most of the peak low-precision</data><data key="conditions">When the workload's operations are dense matrix multiplies in input and accumulation formats the unit supports.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1745" source="mlsys.topology-aware-placement" target="mlsys.network-topology"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">places communicating workers according to</data><data key="conditions">When the scheduler holds a map of links, switches, and scale-up domains and the job declares which workers communicate most.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1746" source="mlsys.quantization-aware-training" target="mlsys.integer-only-inference"><data key="relation">enables</data><data key="statement">makes accurate, by simulating rounding and requantization during training,</data><data key="conditions">When the simulated quantization matches the deployed integer arithmetic and requantization rules.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1747" source="mlsys.embedding-cache" target="mlsys.memory-footprint"><data key="relation">reduces</data><data key="statement">keeps only hot embedding rows resident, shrinking the accelerator-side</data><data key="conditions">When footprint is measured in accelerator memory and lookup skew lets cold rows live in host memory or SSD; total stored model bytes are unchanged.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1748" source="mlsys.dataset-shuffling" target="mlsys.input-pipeline"><data key="relation">part-of</data><data key="statement">is the ordering stage of</data><data key="conditions">When examples are read in a randomized order rather than in stored order.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1749" source="mlsys.dataset-shuffling" target="mlsys.streaming-dataset-format"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">shuffles shards, then records within a buffer, from</data><data key="conditions">When the corpus is stored as sharded files so a two-level shuffle approximates a global permutation.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1750" source="mlsys.collectives" target="mlsys.training-power-swings"><data key="relation">causes</data><data key="statement">idles every accelerator in lockstep during exposed, producing</data><data key="conditions">When collectives are synchronous and not overlapped, so the whole cluster alternates between full-power compute and low-power waiting at the same moments.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1751" source="mlsys.peer-to-peer-transfer" target="mlsys.scale-up-interconnect"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">moves data between accelerators over</data><data key="conditions">When transfers are carried by dedicated accelerator-to-accelerator scale-up links rather than over the host link.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1752" source="mlsys.overfitting" target="mlsys.generalization"><data key="relation">reduces</data><data key="statement">widens the gap between training and held-out performance, lowering</data><data key="conditions">When the model has capacity to fit training-set noise and training continues past the point where held-out performance peaks.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1753" source="mlsys.regularization" target="mlsys.overfitting"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">mitigates</data><data key="conditions">When the penalty or procedure limits the model's ability to fit training-set noise.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1754" source="mlsys.training-diagnostics" target="mlsys.overfitting"><data key="relation">detects</data><data key="statement">reveals, through diverging training and validation curves,</data><data key="conditions">When validation metrics are logged alongside training loss at step granularity.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1755" source="mlsys.overfitting" target="mlsys.membership-inference"><data key="relation">increases</data><data key="statement">raises the train-versus-nontrain confidence gap exploited by</data><data key="conditions">When overfitting creates train-versus-nontrain confidence or loss differences that the attack exploits, raising its advantage.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1756" source="mlsys.predictive-uncertainty" target="mlsys.selective-prediction"><data key="relation">enables</data><data key="statement">supplies the confidence signal that decides abstention in</data><data key="conditions">When the estimate ranks errors well enough that abstaining on the least certain inputs lowers risk at the chosen coverage.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1757" source="mlsys.active-learning" target="mlsys.predictive-uncertainty"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">selects examples to label from</data><data key="conditions">For uncertainty-sampling acquisition functions.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1758" source="mlsys.ood-detection" target="mlsys.predictive-uncertainty"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">thresholds epistemic signals from</data><data key="conditions">When uncertainty rises on inputs far from the training data; raw softmax confidence is often overconfident there.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1759" source="mlsys.statistical-power" target="mlsys.online-experiment"><data key="relation">determines</data><data key="statement">sets the traffic share and duration required by</data><data key="conditions">When the launch decision must detect an effect of a stated minimum size at stated error rates; metric variance and the number of independent units fix the answer.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1760" source="mlsys.paired-comparison" target="mlsys.statistical-power"><data key="relation">increases</data><data key="statement">raises, by cancelling shared item variance,</data><data key="conditions">When both systems are scored on the same items so per-item differences vary less than independent samples.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1761" source="mlsys.statistical-uncertainty" target="mlsys.statistical-power"><data key="relation">reduces</data><data key="statement">lowers, through outcome variance,</data><data key="conditions">At a fixed sample size; a noisier metric needs more units to detect the same effect.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1762" source="mlsys.retrieval-metrics" target="mlsys.metrics"><data key="relation">specializes</data><data key="statement">is the ranked-list family of</data><data key="conditions">When outputs are ranked candidate lists judged against relevance labels.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1763" source="mlsys.retrieval-metrics" target="mlsys.retrieval"><data key="relation">measures</data><data key="statement">scores the ranked results of</data><data key="conditions">When relevance judgments exist for the query set; judged pools miss relevant unjudged records.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1764" source="mlsys.retrieval-metrics" target="mlsys.ann"><data key="relation">measures</data><data key="statement">quantifies, as recall against exact search, the approximation loss of</data><data key="conditions">When ground-truth neighbors are computed by exhaustive search on the same corpus.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1765" source="mlsys.rejection-sampling-fine-tuning" target="mlsys.supervised-fine-tuning"><data key="relation">specializes</data><data key="statement">is the self-generated-data form of</data><data key="conditions">When the training targets are the model's own accepted samples rather than human demonstrations.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1766" source="mlsys.rejection-sampling-fine-tuning" target="mlsys.verifier"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">filters candidates with</data><data key="conditions">When acceptance is decided by a programmatic check, a reward model, or a grader; filter errors become training labels.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1767" source="mlsys.rejection-sampling-fine-tuning" target="mlsys.rlvr"><data key="relation">alternative-to</data><data key="statement">is an offline, SFT-based alternative to</data><data key="conditions">Both learn from verifiable outcomes; rejection sampling updates only on accepted samples while RLVR takes policy gradients over all sampled outcomes.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1768" source="mlsys.rejection-sampling-fine-tuning" target="mlsys.mode-collapse"><data key="relation">causes</data><data key="statement">narrows sampled diversity across rounds toward</data><data key="conditions">When successive rounds train only on the model's own high-scoring samples, concentrating training mass on a narrowing response family without fresh human or real data.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1769" source="mlsys.early-stopping" target="mlsys.regularization"><data key="relation">specializes</data><data key="statement">acts, by truncating training, as a form of</data><data key="conditions">When validation performance peaks before the training loss plateaus.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1770" source="mlsys.early-stopping" target="mlsys.evaluation-split"><data key="relation">requires</data><data key="statement">monitors a validation partition from</data><data key="conditions">The stopping decision must not use the final test partition, or the reported score is optimistically biased.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1771" source="mlsys.experiment-budget" target="mlsys.early-stopping"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">stops unpromising trials early under</data><data key="conditions">When a trial is abandoned once its validation metric plateaus, freeing its remaining budget for other configurations.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1772" source="mlsys.group-dro" target="mlsys.loss"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">optimizes a worst-group aggregation of</data><data key="conditions">When examples carry group labels and training minimizes the highest group loss rather than the mean.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1773" source="mlsys.group-dro" target="mlsys.distribution-shift"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">protects worst-group performance under subpopulation forms of</data><data key="conditions">When the groups that dominate at deployment are among those declared at training.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1774" source="mlsys.loss-masking" target="mlsys.chat-template"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">locates assistant spans through</data><data key="conditions">When non-target tokens are identified by role markers in the rendered sequence.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1775" source="mlsys.chat-template" target="mlsys.train-serve-skew"><data key="relation">causes</data><data key="statement">causes, when rendered differently in training and serving,</data><data key="conditions">When the serving stack renders turns with different markers or whitespace from the training renderer.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1776" source="mlsys.chat-template" target="mlsys.tokenization"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">reserves special tokens in</data><data key="conditions">When role markers are single reserved tokens rather than plain text.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1777" source="mlsys.generation-stopping" target="mlsys.kv-cache"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">caps per-request growth of</data><data key="conditions">A server-enforced output-token cap limits additional retained token positions in engines whose autoregressive cache grows with generation; it does not bound all allocated cache memory.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1778" source="mlsys.generation-stopping" target="mlsys.tail-latency"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">limits the output-length contribution to</data><data key="conditions">Decode-step time is bounded, while queueing, stalls, and retries are accounted for separately.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1779" source="mlsys.generation-stopping" target="mlsys.resource-exhaustion-attack"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">caps the output tokens one request can consume, blunting</data><data key="conditions">A server-enforced output cap limits generated-token work per request; separate controls are needed for prompt processing, request rate, and aggregate client consumption.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1780" source="mlsys.runbook" target="mlsys.incident-response"><data key="relation">part-of</data><data key="statement">scripts the first steps of</data><data key="conditions">When the failure has been seen before and its mitigation is known.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1781" source="mlsys.alerting" target="mlsys.runbook"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">links each page to a</data><data key="conditions">When alerts are actionable and name the procedure to follow.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1782" source="mlsys.postmortem" target="mlsys.runbook"><data key="relation">produces</data><data key="statement">writes or revises a</data><data key="conditions">When corrective actions include documenting the response for next time.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1783" source="mlsys.runbook" target="mlsys.rollback"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">often names, as the first mitigation,</data><data key="conditions">For model-serving incidents where the previous version is known good.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1784" source="mlsys.rl-training-colocation" target="mlsys.weight-sync"><data key="relation">determines</data><data key="statement">sets the transfer path and cost of</data><data key="conditions">Colocated designs reshard in device memory; disaggregated ones send weights across the network.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1785" source="mlsys.rl-training-colocation" target="mlsys.dynamic-resharding"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">switches between training and generation layouts with</data><data key="conditions">When the colocated design runs both phases on one device set.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1786" source="mlsys.rl-training-colocation" target="mlsys.policy-staleness"><data key="relation">increases</data><data key="statement">raises, in its disaggregated asynchronous form,</data><data key="conditions">When rollout workers keep generating while trainers update.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1787" source="mlsys.sequential-revision" target="mlsys.best-of-n"><data key="relation">alternative-to</data><data key="statement">spends compute serially rather than in parallel samples as in</data><data key="conditions">When one task's inference budget is allocated between dependent revisions and independent candidates.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1788" source="mlsys.sequential-revision" target="mlsys.verifier"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">benefits from external feedback supplied by</data><data key="conditions">When the critique comes from a checker rather than the model's own judgment, which is often unreliable.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1789" source="mlsys.sequential-revision" target="mlsys.tail-latency"><data key="relation">increases</data><data key="statement">adds serial passes to</data><data key="conditions">Each pass must finish before the next begins.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1790" source="mlsys.semi-supervised-learning" target="mlsys.supervised-learning"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">trains on labeled and pseudo-labeled examples with</data><data key="conditions">After unlabeled examples receive model-assigned targets.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1791" source="mlsys.semi-supervised-learning" target="mlsys.weak-supervision"><data key="relation">alternative-to</data><data key="statement">derives extra labels from the model itself rather than from heuristics as in</data><data key="conditions">Both reduce manual labeling; semi-supervision needs a labeled seed and a model confident enough to self-label.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1792" source="mlsys.semi-supervised-learning" target="mlsys.selective-prediction"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">keeps only confident pseudo-labels through</data><data key="conditions">When a confidence threshold decides which unlabeled examples enter training.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1793" source="mlsys.contrastive-learning" target="mlsys.embeddings"><data key="relation">produces</data><data key="statement">trains the encoders behind</data><data key="conditions">When the learned representation is reused for retrieval, classification heads, or multimodal alignment.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1794" source="mlsys.contrastive-learning" target="mlsys.dense-retrieval"><data key="relation">enables</data><data key="statement">trains the bi-encoders used by</data><data key="conditions">When query-document pairs supply positives and in-batch items supply negatives.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1795" source="mlsys.batch-size" target="mlsys.contrastive-learning"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">bounds the in-batch negatives available to</data><data key="conditions">When negatives are drawn from the same batch rather than a memory bank.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1796" source="mlsys.training-memory-estimate" target="mlsys.training-state"><data key="relation">measures</data><data key="statement">sizes</data><data key="conditions">For dense models where each component's precision and the optimizer's state count are known.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1797" source="mlsys.training-memory-estimate" target="mlsys.precision"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">takes byte widths from</data><data key="conditions">Each state component may use a different format.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1798" source="mlsys.training-memory-estimate" target="mlsys.memory-footprint"><data key="relation">measures</data><data key="statement">estimates the training share of</data><data key="conditions">Runtime buffers and allocator overhead are added separately.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1799" source="mlsys.rl-rollout" target="mlsys.continuous-batching"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">generates many sampled responses per prompt through</data><data key="conditions">When rollouts are produced by a serving engine rather than the training framework, so engine throughput bounds samples per step.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1800" source="mlsys.retraining" target="mlsys.model-artifact"><data key="relation">produces</data><data key="statement">yields a replacement</data><data key="conditions">After the retrained model passes validation and is versioned for release.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1801" source="mlsys.hyperparameter-search" target="mlsys.experiment-budget"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">allocates trials under</data><data key="conditions">Whenever the number of configurations exceeds what can be trained to completion, so trials, repetitions, and fidelities compete for one budget.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1802" source="mlsys.continuous-training" target="mlsys.data-validation"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">validates incoming data before each run of</data><data key="conditions">When the pipeline is automated end to end, so a schema or distribution failure must stop retraining rather than a person.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1803" source="mlsys.model-gateway" target="mlsys.admission"><data key="relation">implements</data><data key="statement">enforces per-client rate limits as</data><data key="conditions">When all model calls pass through the gateway and limits are expressed per key, tenant, or application.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1804" source="mlsys.alerting" target="mlsys.drift-detection"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">fires on thresholds over statistics from</data><data key="conditions">When drift statistics are alerted on directly; detected drift is a signal to diagnose, not proof of degraded quality.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1805" source="mlsys.kv-cache-offload" target="mlsys.kv-cache-eviction"><data key="relation">alternative-to</data><data key="statement">keeps cold entries recoverable instead of discarding them as in</data><data key="conditions">Both bound resident KV memory; offload spends transfer bandwidth to preserve exactness, eviction spends quality to avoid transfers; they can be combined.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1806" source="mlsys.pass-at-k" target="mlsys.test-oracle"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">counts a sample as correct according to a</data><data key="conditions">When the check is unit tests or a programmatic grader; the metric inherits the oracle's false positives.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1807" source="mlsys.failure-attribution" target="mlsys.inference-logging"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">reconstructs the failing request from</data><data key="conditions">When logs retain inputs, model version, and outputs for the incident window.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1808" source="mlsys.privacy-budget" target="mlsys.differential-privacy"><data key="relation">part-of</data><data key="statement">is the accounting side of</data><data key="conditions">When several releases or training steps on the same data must be composed into one guarantee.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1809" source="mlsys.dp-sgd" target="mlsys.privacy-budget"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">tracks its noise multiplier, sampling rate, and step count through</data><data key="conditions">At every optimizer step; the accountant decides when training must stop.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1810" source="mlsys.privacy-budget" target="mlsys.retraining"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">caps how many times private data can be reused by</data><data key="conditions">When each retraining run spends from a fixed budget rather than resetting it.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1811" source="mlsys.bias-sources" target="mlsys.bias-mitigation"><data key="relation">determines</data><data key="statement">selects which stage of intervention is appropriate in</data><data key="conditions">When the dominant source is identified, since reweighting cannot fix a measurement problem and a constraint cannot fix a deployment mismatch.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1812" source="mlsys.bias-sources" target="mlsys.proxy-discrimination"><data key="relation">causes</data><data key="statement">can produce, through correlated features,</data><data key="conditions">When historical or measurement bias is encoded in features correlated with protected attributes and those features drive decisions.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1813" source="mlsys.carbon-accounting-method" target="mlsys.emission-scopes"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">applies to the purchased-electricity category of</data><data key="conditions">When purchased-electricity emissions are identified as Scope 2 before the attribution method is chosen.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1814" source="mlsys.carbon-accounting-method" target="mlsys.operational-carbon"><data key="relation">measures</data><data key="statement">reports, under declared factors and boundaries, the electricity share of</data><data key="conditions">When contractual instruments cover a material share of consumption, so the market-based figure diverges from the location-based one.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1815" source="mlsys.retry-backoff" target="mlsys.request-timeout"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">treats as its failure signal the expiry of</data><data key="conditions">When a retry is issued on timeout rather than on an explicit error; the retry budget and the deadline must be set together.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1816" source="mlsys.request-timeout" target="mlsys.metastable-failure"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">cancels abandoned work that would otherwise sustain</data><data key="conditions">When deadlines are propagated so downstream services stop serving requests the client no longer waits for.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1817" source="mlsys.circuit-breaker" target="mlsys.request-timeout"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">counts expirations as errors in</data><data key="conditions">When slow responses, not only explicit failures, trip the breaker.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1818" source="mlsys.data-protection-regulation" target="mlsys.data-consent"><data key="relation">requires</data><data key="statement">mandates a lawful basis and purpose limitation enforced through</data><data key="conditions">When personal data of covered people is processed for training or inference.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1819" source="mlsys.data-protection-regulation" target="mlsys.deletion-propagation"><data key="relation">requires</data><data key="statement">creates erasure obligations fulfilled by</data><data key="conditions">When a legally effective erasure obligation covers the retained personal data and its derived copies.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1820" source="mlsys.data-protection-regulation" target="mlsys.privacy"><data key="relation">determines</data><data key="statement">sets the legal floor for</data><data key="conditions">Within the jurisdictions whose law applies to the people in the data.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1821" source="mlsys.egress-control" target="mlsys.weight-exfiltration"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">blocks the outbound channels used for</data><data key="conditions">When weights can leave only through network paths the allowlist controls.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1822" source="mlsys.confidential-computing" target="mlsys.weight-exfiltration"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">keeps weights unreadable to the host operator, limiting</data><data key="conditions">When the weights are decrypted only inside the attested environment.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1823" source="mlsys.weight-exfiltration" target="mlsys.model-extraction"><data key="relation">alternative-to</data><data key="statement">obtains the model directly instead of reconstructing it by queries as in</data><data key="conditions">Both yield a usable copy; theft needs infrastructure access, extraction needs only the prediction interface.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1824" source="mlsys.ai-bill-of-materials" target="mlsys.supply-chain-compromise"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">lets consumers find known-bad components behind</data><data key="conditions">When the manifest is complete and machine-readable and components flagged against advisories are rejected, replaced, or contained; the inventory alone reveals but does not prevent a compromise.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1825" source="mlsys.ai-bill-of-materials" target="mlsys.lineage"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">is compiled from</data><data key="conditions">When data lineage records the sources and transformations the manifest lists.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1826" source="mlsys.artifact-signing" target="mlsys.ai-bill-of-materials"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">binds to the weights, through its signature, the contents of</data><data key="conditions">When the signed payload includes the manifest rather than only the weight bytes.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1827" source="mlsys.risk-tiered-regulation" target="mlsys.ai-incident-reporting"><data key="relation">requires</data><data key="statement">obliges providers of high-risk systems to perform</data><data key="conditions">For serious incidents under the governing regulation, within its reporting deadlines.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1828" source="mlsys.ai-risk-management" target="mlsys.ai-incident-reporting"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">feeds its measure and manage functions from</data><data key="conditions">When reported incidents are reviewed against the risk register rather than only closed.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1829" source="mlsys.ai-incident-reporting" target="mlsys.postmortem"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">draws its causal account from</data><data key="conditions">When the incident was handled by an internal incident process before disclosure.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1830" source="mlsys.mechanistic-interpretability" target="mlsys.feature-attribution"><data key="relation">alternative-to</data><data key="statement">explains internal computation rather than input contributions, unlike</data><data key="conditions">Both aim at understanding a decision; attribution scores inputs, mechanistic methods inspect representations and need model access.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1831" source="mlsys.mechanistic-interpretability" target="mlsys.linear-model"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">fits linear probes from</data><data key="conditions">For probing, where a linear classifier on activations tests whether a property is linearly represented.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1832" source="mlsys.safety-certification" target="mlsys.safety-case"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">assesses the argument and evidence in</data><data key="conditions">Under standards that require a safety case; others prescribe process evidence directly.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1833" source="mlsys.change-impact-analysis" target="mlsys.safety-certification"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">scopes re-assessment against the claims certified by</data><data key="conditions">When a model, data, or operating-domain change touches certified claims.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1834" source="mlsys.hazard-analysis" target="mlsys.tolerable-risk"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">ranks identified hazards against</data><data key="conditions">When the analysis must decide which hazards receive controls rather than only list them.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1835" source="mlsys.safety-case" target="mlsys.tolerable-risk"><data key="relation">requires</data><data key="statement">argues acceptability against</data><data key="conditions">The top claim of a safety case is that residual risk is acceptable, which is undefined without the criteria.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1836" source="mlsys.fleet-hardware-screening" target="mlsys.silent-data-corruption"><data key="relation">detects</data><data key="statement">finds the units responsible for</data><data key="conditions">For faults that reproduce under the test workload; intermittent and workload-dependent faults need longer or in-production screening.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1837" source="mlsys.fleet-hardware-screening" target="mlsys.test-oracle"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">compares outputs against</data><data key="conditions">When reference outputs are computed on trusted hardware or by a redundant run.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1838" source="mlsys.fleet-hardware-screening" target="mlsys.utilization"><data key="relation">trades-off</data><data key="statement">spends fleet time that would otherwise count toward</data><data key="conditions">When screening runs on production capacity rather than on units already out of service.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1839" source="mlsys.profiling" target="mlsys.hardware-performance-counters"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">reads stall and throughput causes from</data><data key="conditions">When the profiler collects counters in addition to timestamps; collection can perturb execution and on some accelerators serializes it.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1840" source="mlsys.bottleneck-analysis" target="mlsys.hardware-performance-counters"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">places a kernel on the roofline using measured bytes and operations from</data><data key="conditions">When measured rather than estimated arithmetic intensity is needed.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1841" source="mlsys.structured-pruning" target="mlsys.nm-sparsity"><data key="relation">alternative-to</data><data key="statement">removes whole units for dense hardware instead of the fine per-group pattern of</data><data key="conditions">Both aim at real speedup from pruning; structured pruning needs no sparse-engine support but usually costs more quality at equal parameter reduction, N:M needs a matrix engine with the matching sparse instruction.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1842" source="mlsys.gray-failure" target="mlsys.health-checks"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">passes undetected through</data><data key="conditions">When the degraded node still answers liveness and readiness probes while failing the work callers send it.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1843" source="mlsys.over-refusal" target="mlsys.jailbreak"><data key="relation">trades-off</data><data key="statement">is the false-positive side of the operating point whose false-negative side is</data><data key="conditions">When a single threshold or refusal policy decides both, so loosening it to admit benign requests also admits more successful bypasses.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1844" source="mlsys.membership-inference" target="mlsys.machine-unlearning"><data key="relation">measures</data><data key="statement">audits whether removed records still influence a model after</data><data key="conditions">When attack advantage on the forgotten records, compared with a model retrained without them, is used as the empirical test of forgetting.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1845" source="mlsys.guardrails" target="mlsys.tail-latency"><data key="relation">increases</data><data key="statement">adds classifier passes on the request path to</data><data key="conditions">When input and output checks run synchronously before the response is released; streaming output checks shift the cost to time-to-last-token.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1846" source="mlsys.silent-data-corruption" target="mlsys.training-instability"><data key="relation">causes</data><data key="statement">can inject the corrupted gradients or weights behind</data><data key="conditions">When a miscomputed value propagates into optimizer state before any check catches it, so a loss spike or divergence appears with no software cause.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1847" source="mlsys.stragglers" target="mlsys.training-goodput"><data key="relation">reduces</data><data key="statement">idles every other rank, lowering</data><data key="conditions">In synchronous training, where each step waits for the slowest rank; asynchronous or straggler-tolerant schemes break the dependence.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1848" source="mlsys.human-oversight" target="mlsys.selective-prediction"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">routes abstained cases to people through</data><data key="conditions">When the system abstains on low-confidence inputs and a human review path exists with enough time and context to decide.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1849" source="mlsys.agent-harness" target="mlsys.trajectory-log"><data key="relation">produces</data><data key="statement">writes, as the sole author, the</data><data key="conditions">When the harness, not the model, owns continuation and records each step before dispatching it.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1850" source="mlsys.durable-execution" target="mlsys.trajectory-log"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">recovers progress and reuses recorded results from the</data><data key="conditions">When each step's intent is appended before the effect is issued, so a crash between the two can be reconciled.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1851" source="mlsys.deterministic-replay" target="mlsys.trajectory-log"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">substitutes recorded model outputs and tool results from the</data><data key="conditions">When the log contains every input needed for the declared replay boundary.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1852" source="mlsys.agent-evaluation" target="mlsys.trajectory-log"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">scores intermediate effects and constraint compliance from the</data><data key="conditions">When evaluation reads the record of what was dispatched rather than only the final answer.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1853" source="mlsys.odometry" target="mlsys.state-estimation"><data key="relation">specializes</data><data key="statement">is the relative-motion, drift-accumulating form of</data><data key="conditions">Only relative motion is measured, so absolute pose is unobservable without an external reference.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1854" source="mlsys.slam" target="mlsys.odometry"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">takes its motion prior between keyframes from</data><data key="conditions">When the front end propagates pose with odometry and the back end corrects drift at loop closure.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1855" source="mlsys.recursive-filtering" target="mlsys.odometry"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">propagates the predicted state in the prediction step with</data><data key="conditions">When odometry supplies the control input to a Kalman-family or particle filter.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1856" source="mlsys.odometry" target="mlsys.coordinate-frames"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">accumulates pose in the odometry frame defined by</data><data key="conditions">In systems that keep a continuous odometry frame beneath a map frame, so the map-to-odometry transform absorbs drift correction.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1857" source="mlsys.capture-epoch" target="mlsys.observation-age"><data key="relation">determines</data><data key="statement">fixes the start instant of</data><data key="conditions">Observation age is measured from the capture epoch, not from the frame's arrival at the processor.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1858" source="mlsys.capture-epoch" target="mlsys.sensor-fusion"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">misaligns measurements taken at different instants within</data><data key="conditions">When the platform or scene moves during exposure or a scan and the measurements are not de-skewed before fusion.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1859" source="mlsys.recursive-filtering" target="mlsys.capture-epoch"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">applies each measurement at its</data><data key="conditions">When measurements arrive out of order or late, the filter must correct at the capture epoch rather than at arrival.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1860" source="mlsys.timing-jitter" target="mlsys.feedback-control"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">degrades, by varying the sample interval of,</data><data key="conditions">When the controller's gains assume a fixed period and the actual release or completion time varies.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1861" source="mlsys.real-time-scheduling" target="mlsys.timing-jitter"><data key="relation">reduces</data><data key="statement">bounds, through fixed priorities and periodic release,</data><data key="conditions">When the control task has the highest priority or is released by a timer rather than by a best-effort scheduler.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1862" source="mlsys.xil-testing" target="mlsys.timing-jitter"><data key="relation">measures</data><data key="statement">exposes, on the real compute and bus,</data><data key="conditions">When the real software and hardware run in the loop; pure simulation hides it.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1863" source="mlsys.thermal-dynamics" target="mlsys.timing-jitter"><data key="relation">increases</data><data key="statement">adds, through frequency throttling,</data><data key="conditions">When clock changes under thermal policy alter task completion times between periods.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1864" source="mlsys.deterministic-field-network" target="mlsys.feedback-delay"><data key="relation">increases</data><data key="statement">adds a bounded transport and cycle-time term to</data><data key="conditions">When drives and sensors are reached over the bus rather than on-chip.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1865" source="mlsys.robotics-middleware" target="mlsys.deterministic-field-network"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">reaches drives and encoders through</data><data key="conditions">When middleware nodes talk to actuators and sensors over a fieldbus rather than directly.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1866" source="mlsys.deterministic-field-network" target="mlsys.real-time-scheduling"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">bounds, by bus occupancy, the achievable period of</data><data key="conditions">When the control task's period must fit the bus cycle and the number of devices on it.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1867" source="mlsys.feedback-delay" target="mlsys.loop-stability-margin"><data key="relation">reduces</data><data key="statement">spends, at a rate set by the loop's bandwidth,</data><data key="conditions">Each unit of delay costs phase in proportion to the crossover frequency.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1868" source="mlsys.loop-stability-margin" target="mlsys.feedback-control"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">caps the achievable bandwidth and gain of</data><data key="conditions">When the controller is tuned to keep a stated margin under the worst-case delay and plant variation.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1869" source="mlsys.dynamics" target="mlsys.loop-stability-margin"><data key="relation">determines</data><data key="statement">sets, together with the controller, the</data><data key="conditions">The margin is a property of the plant-controller pair, not of the controller alone.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1870" source="mlsys.impedance-force-control" target="mlsys.feedback-control"><data key="relation">specializes</data><data key="statement">is the contact-regulating form of</data><data key="conditions">The regulated variable is the force-motion relationship rather than position alone.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1871" source="mlsys.impedance-force-control" target="mlsys.contact-compliance"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">models the environment's stiffness and damping through</data><data key="conditions">Stable contact depends on the combined compliance of robot, controller, and environment.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1872" source="mlsys.feedback-delay" target="mlsys.impedance-force-control"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">destabilizes stiff contact under</data><data key="conditions">When commanded stiffness is high and the loop delay is long relative to the contact dynamics.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1873" source="mlsys.safety-filter" target="mlsys.impedance-force-control"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">bounds contact force at the actuator through</data><data key="conditions">When the filter's safe action is expressed as a force or stiffness limit rather than a position correction.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1874" source="mlsys.authorization" target="mlsys.tool-contract"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">checks a proposed tool call against the authority and effects declared in the</data><data key="conditions">When tool calls are authorized per action from the contract rather than by ambient session rights; schema conformance alone grants nothing.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1875" source="mlsys.sensor-interference" target="mlsys.sensor-degradation"><data key="relation">specializes</data><data key="statement">is the multi-emitter form of</data><data key="conditions">When other active sensors share the scene; the hardware has not failed and the loss of fidelity depends on the operating environment.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1876" source="mlsys.sensor-interconnect" target="mlsys.robot-perception"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">bounds the resolution, frame rate, and sensor count available to</data><data key="conditions">When raw sensor data streams into the onboard processor over MIPI, I2S, or SPI links rather than being pre-processed at the sensor.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1877" source="mlsys.sensor-degradation" target="mlsys.robot-perception"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">degrades the inputs to</data><data key="conditions">When the perception model was trained and validated on clean conditions and no input-support monitor flags the degraded regime.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1878" source="mlsys.approval-gate" target="mlsys.agent-harness"><data key="relation">part-of</data><data key="statement">is the undispatched hold within</data><data key="conditions">When the harness holds the proposed action, bound by digest and expiry, until an authorized decision, and re-runs admission after approval.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1879" source="mlsys.agent-interruption" target="mlsys.agent-harness"><data key="relation">requires</data><data key="statement">takes effect only at turn boundaries enforced by the</data><data key="conditions">When a runtime owns continuation and dispatch, enforces cancellation at defined execution boundaries, and separately settles already issued external actions.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1880" source="mlsys.diffusion-policy" target="mlsys.action-tokenization"><data key="relation">alternative-to</data><data key="statement">generates continuous actions by denoising rather than through the discretization of</data><data key="conditions">When choosing a robot policy's action head: tokens trade reconstruction error and decode steps against expressiveness; denoising trades inference iterations against multimodality.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1881" source="mlsys.vla-model" target="mlsys.diffusion-policy"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">can emit continuous actions through a</data><data key="conditions">When the action head denoises or integrates a learned flow instead of decoding action tokens (pi0 and its successors).</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1882" source="mlsys.vla-model" target="mlsys.cross-embodiment-training"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">is commonly pretrained with</data><data key="conditions">When the policy is pretrained on pooled multi-robot datasets before task fine-tuning.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1883" source="mlsys.closed-loop-evaluation" target="mlsys.sim-to-real"><data key="relation">measures</data><data key="statement">scores, by physical trial success rate,</data><data key="conditions">When comparable closed-loop simulation and physical trials give an explicit transfer comparison under matched task and measurement conditions.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1884" source="mlsys.multi-agent-orchestration" target="mlsys.capability-based-security"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">attenuates delegated rights to each worker through</data><data key="conditions">When a worker agent must receive narrower authority than its orchestrator.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1885" source="mlsys.multi-agent-orchestration" target="mlsys.agentic-workload"><data key="relation">increases</data><data key="statement">multiplies, by duplicating context across workers,</data><data key="conditions">When each delegated agent carries its own context and makes its own dependent model calls, so total tokens exceed a single-agent run on the same task.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1886" source="mlsys.context-selection" target="mlsys.agent-memory"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">draws recalled records into the working set from</data><data key="conditions">When the agent has persistent memory; the recalled record competes for the same token budget as instructions, tool descriptions, and observations.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1887" source="mlsys.thermal-dynamics" target="mlsys.wcet"><data key="relation">determines</data><data key="statement">fixes the clock and memory-timing assumptions under which a bound is a valid</data><data key="conditions">When the bound was derived at full clock; the analysis must include the allowed throttled operating states or explicitly exclude them.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1888" source="mlsys.power-delivery" target="mlsys.actuator-limits"><data key="relation">determines</data><data key="statement">sets, when a shared rail sags, the torque available within</data><data key="conditions">When compute and actuators share a supply and simultaneous demand or regeneration droops the rail below the drive's operating window.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1889" source="mlsys.thermal-dynamics" target="mlsys.actuator-limits"><data key="relation">determines</data><data key="statement">sets the sustained-duty component of</data><data key="conditions">When motor winding temperature, not drive current, binds continuous torque; peak torque remains current-limited.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1890" source="mlsys.sim-to-real" target="mlsys.parallel-simulation"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">starts from policies trained in</data><data key="conditions">When the policy's experience came from batched simulated environments rather than physical collection.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1891" source="mlsys.idempotency" target="mlsys.in-doubt-action"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">makes safe the retry of an</data><data key="conditions">When the operation carries an identity the endpoint deduplicates.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1892" source="mlsys.agent-interruption" target="mlsys.in-doubt-action"><data key="relation">causes</data><data key="statement">leaves, when it cancels mid-dispatch, an</data><data key="conditions">When a tool call was sent before the cancel and its result never arrived.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1893" source="mlsys.in-doubt-action" target="mlsys.compensation"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">cannot be compensated until it is known to have happened, so it bounds</data><data key="conditions">A compensating action for an effect that never occurred is itself a new effect.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1894" source="mlsys.model-bandwidth-utilization" target="mlsys.model-flops-utilization"><data key="relation">alternative-to</data><data key="statement">is the memory-bound counterpart of</data><data key="conditions">When one primary efficiency indicator is chosen for a stated bottleneck; both ratios can be reported for the same workload, and the bound regime says which one explains the ceiling.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1895" source="mlsys.model-bandwidth-utilization" target="mlsys.tpot"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">divides bytes per token by the measured</data><data key="conditions">When achieved bandwidth is estimated from weight and KV-cache bytes over time per output token rather than from hardware counters.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1896" source="mlsys.hot-spare-capacity" target="mlsys.training-goodput"><data key="relation">increases</data><data key="statement">raises, by shortening node-replacement waits, the</data><data key="conditions">When reduced recovery and rework losses exceed the reserve time charged to the same goodput denominator.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1897" source="mlsys.hot-spare-capacity" target="mlsys.utilization"><data key="relation">trades-off</data><data key="statement">holds idle capacity that lowers fleet</data><data key="conditions">When fleet utilization counts the reserved idle spares in its capacity denominator.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1898" source="mlsys.hot-spare-capacity" target="mlsys.elastic-training"><data key="relation">alternative-to</data><data key="statement">replaces a failed worker instead of shrinking the job, unlike</data><data key="conditions">When both replacement at the original world size and continuation at a reduced world size are feasible recovery choices.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1899" source="mlsys.hot-spare-capacity" target="mlsys.mtbf"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">uses the failure interval summarized by</data><data key="conditions">When sizing converts component or job MTBF into a failure-arrival model and combines it with repair or replenishment times, failure correlations, and an acceptable spare-exhaustion probability.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1900" source="mlsys.geo-distributed-training" target="mlsys.local-sgd"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">cuts cross-site synchronization frequency with</data><data key="conditions">When inter-site bandwidth cannot carry per-step gradient exchange.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1901" source="mlsys.geo-distributed-training" target="mlsys.pipeline-parallel"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">places stage boundaries on inter-site links using</data><data key="conditions">When per-micro-batch activations are far smaller than the gradients a data-parallel cut would send across the WAN.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1902" source="mlsys.network-propagation-delay" target="mlsys.geo-distributed-training"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">sets a lower bound on each synchronous exchange of</data><data key="conditions">When sites are hundreds of kilometers apart, so the round trip bounds every cross-site exchange regardless of bandwidth.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1903" source="mlsys.geo-distributed-training" target="mlsys.hybrid-parallel"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">extends to the datacenter tier the hierarchy mapping of</data><data key="conditions">When the hardware hierarchy runs chip, node, rack, and site, and each dimension is matched to a tier's link speed.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1904" source="mlsys.quarantined-model-pattern" target="mlsys.prompt-injection"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">removes the channel from untrusted text to privileged actions used by</data><data key="conditions">When the planner and runtime are trusted, untrusted content stays isolated in the quarantined model, and runtime policies validate derived arguments and data flows before any privileged effect.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1905" source="mlsys.quarantined-model-pattern" target="mlsys.information-flow-control"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">tags quarantined outputs and checks tool arguments with</data><data key="conditions">When a value derived from untrusted data must be policy-checked before it reaches a side-effecting tool.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1906" source="mlsys.sycophancy-evaluation" target="mlsys.reward-hacking"><data key="relation">detects</data><data key="statement">surfaces agreement-seeking learned as a form of</data><data key="conditions">When preference training rewarded user approval and the evaluation shows agreement rising while task correctness falls for the same behavior.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1907" source="mlsys.sycophancy-evaluation" target="mlsys.robustness-evaluation"><data key="relation">specializes</data><data key="statement">is a kind of</data><data key="conditions">When the perturbation is conversational pressure rather than input corruption.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1908" source="mlsys.sycophancy-evaluation" target="mlsys.llm-judge"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">scores free-form multi-turn answers with</data><data key="conditions">When responses are open-ended, so a flip cannot be read off a fixed label.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1909" source="mlsys.communication-intensity" target="mlsys.parallel-efficiency"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">together with link bandwidth bounds</data><data key="conditions">When the exchange is not fully overlapped with computation.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1910" source="mlsys.gradient-compression" target="mlsys.communication-intensity"><data key="relation">reduces</data><data key="statement">lowers the bytes per step counted by</data><data key="conditions">When compressed gradients replace full-precision exchange.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1911" source="mlsys.hybrid-parallel" target="mlsys.communication-intensity"><data key="relation">determines</data><data key="statement">sets, per parallelism dimension, the</data><data key="conditions">When the parallel plan fixes which tensors cross which links on each step.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1912" source="mlsys.technology-scaling" target="mlsys.dark-silicon"><data key="relation">causes</data><data key="statement">produces, once constant power density ends,</data><data key="conditions">When transistor count keeps growing but power density no longer falls with feature size.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1913" source="mlsys.technology-scaling" target="mlsys.compute-throughput"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">constrains, once power density stops falling, per-chip gains in</data><data key="conditions">When transistor-density gains lack corresponding voltage and power improvements, so a fixed power budget prevents all added arithmetic units from operating at once at the intended clock rate.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1914" source="mlsys.compression-composition" target="mlsys.pruning"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">typically applies first</data><data key="conditions">When sparsity is introduced before quantization so the quantizer calibrates on the surviving weights.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1915" source="mlsys.compression-composition" target="mlsys.quantization"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">applies to the already-pruned or distilled model</data><data key="conditions">When the bit-width is chosen after structure has been removed.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1916" source="mlsys.compression-composition" target="mlsys.distillation"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">recovers quality lost by the other stages through</data><data key="conditions">When a teacher is available to fine-tune the compressed student.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1917" source="mlsys.compression-composition" target="mlsys.efficiency-frontier"><data key="relation">determines</data><data key="statement">shapes, through the techniques chosen and their order, the attainable</data><data key="conditions">When the frontier is measured over compressed variants rather than architectures.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1918" source="mlsys.fidelity-adaptive-serving" target="mlsys.graceful-degradation"><data key="relation">specializes</data><data key="statement">is a kind of</data><data key="conditions">When the degraded mode is a cheaper model variant rather than a feature cut.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1919" source="mlsys.fidelity-adaptive-serving" target="mlsys.weight-only-quantization"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">obtains its cheaper variants from</data><data key="conditions">When the fallback variant is a lower-precision copy of the same weights.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1920" source="mlsys.compound-workflow-pipelining" target="mlsys.token-streaming"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">connects stages through</data><data key="conditions">When stages exchange streamed tokens rather than complete responses.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1921" source="mlsys.compound-workflow-pipelining" target="mlsys.execution-time"><data key="relation">reduces</data><data key="statement">shortens the end-to-end</data><data key="conditions">When stage dependencies are partial, so the critical path is shorter than the serial sum of stages.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1922" source="mlsys.rag-pipeline-scheduling" target="mlsys.compound-workflow-pipelining"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">overlaps retrieval and generation through</data><data key="conditions">When retrieval or other upstream stages expose stable partial results that downstream generation can process while upstream work continues.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1923" source="mlsys.safety-filter" target="mlsys.reachability-analysis"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">certifies candidate actions against sets computed by</data><data key="conditions">When the filter's safe set is the complement of the backward reachable set of the failure region.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1924" source="mlsys.stopping-envelope" target="mlsys.reachability-analysis"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">is defined from forward reachable sets computed by</data><data key="conditions">When the envelope is the set of states from which a stopping maneuver stays clear of everything reachable by others.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1925" source="mlsys.reachability-analysis" target="mlsys.control-barrier-function"><data key="relation">alternative-to</data><data key="statement">gives a set-based safety certificate as an alternative to</data><data key="conditions">CBFs give local differentiable constraints; reachability gives the safe set itself at the cost of state-space computation.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1926" source="mlsys.reachability-analysis" target="mlsys.dynamics"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">propagates the</data><data key="conditions">Always; the reachable set is defined by the dynamics model and the input and disturbance bounds.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1927" source="mlsys.fairness-monitoring" target="mlsys.group-fairness-metric"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">computes on live traffic the</data><data key="conditions">When protected attributes or accepted proxies are available at monitoring time.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1928" source="mlsys.fairness-monitoring" target="mlsys.monitoring"><data key="relation">specializes</data><data key="statement">is a kind of</data><data key="conditions">When the monitored signal is a disparity across groups rather than an aggregate.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1929" source="mlsys.fairness-monitoring" target="mlsys.sliced-evaluation"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">applies in production the per-group measurement of</data><data key="conditions">When slices are protected groups and the evaluation is continuous rather than a release gate.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1930" source="mlsys.forward-error-correction" target="mlsys.link-signal-integrity"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">absorbs, within its correction capability, the raw errors of</data><data key="conditions">When the error rate stays within the code's correction budget.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1931" source="mlsys.link-signal-integrity" target="mlsys.stragglers"><data key="relation">causes</data><data key="statement">produces retransmission and retraining stalls that appear as</data><data key="conditions">When one link degrades under heat or marginal cabling while the others stay clean.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1932" source="mlsys.link-signal-integrity" target="mlsys.gray-failure"><data key="relation">causes</data><data key="statement">degrades a link that still passes link-up checks, a form of</data><data key="conditions">When flaps are intermittent and health checks sample too coarsely to see them.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1933" source="mlsys.activation-function" target="mlsys.neural-network"><data key="relation">part-of</data><data key="statement">is the nonlinearity between the linear layers of</data><data key="conditions">When the architecture applies pointwise or gated nonlinearities between affine layers, as nearly all do; without them stacked linear layers collapse to one.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1934" source="mlsys.fusion" target="mlsys.activation-function"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">folds into a matrix-multiply epilogue the</data><data key="conditions">When the nonlinearity is elementwise, so it can be applied to tile outputs before they are written to memory.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1935" source="mlsys.activation-function" target="mlsys.activation-sparsity"><data key="relation">enables</data><data key="statement">produces the exact zeros exploited by</data><data key="conditions">When the nonlinearity is ReLU-like; smooth activations such as GELU or SwiGLU need thresholding or prediction to find skippable units.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1936" source="mlsys.attention-ffn-disaggregation" target="mlsys.arithmetic-intensity"><data key="relation">increases</data><data key="statement">pools tokens from many attention instances to raise the expert-side</data><data key="conditions">When the FFN or expert pool batches tokens across several attention replicas, so each weight read serves more tokens.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1937" source="mlsys.attention-ffn-disaggregation" target="mlsys.communication-overlap"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">hides inter-pool activation transfer with</data><data key="conditions">When micro-batches are pipelined so one batch computes while another's activations are in flight.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1938" source="mlsys.attention-ffn-disaggregation" target="mlsys.expert-parallel"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">serves the expert pool with</data><data key="conditions">For mixture-of-experts models whose experts are spread across the FFN pool.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1939" source="mlsys.attention-ffn-disaggregation" target="mlsys.inter-token-latency"><data key="relation">trades-off</data><data key="statement">adds a per-layer activation transfer that lengthens</data><data key="conditions">When the activation exchange between pools is not hidden by ping-pong micro-batching.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1940" source="mlsys.attention-ffn-disaggregation" target="mlsys.heterogeneous-accelerator-execution"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">can place each pool on a different accelerator type through</data><data key="conditions">When attention is deployed on bandwidth-rich devices and FFN on compute-rich devices.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1941" source="mlsys.label-shift-estimation" target="mlsys.distribution-shift"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">corrects, without new labels, the label-shift form of</data><data key="conditions">When class priors change but class-conditional input distributions do not; it does not address covariate or concept drift.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1942" source="mlsys.label-shift-estimation" target="mlsys.calibration"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">adjusts posteriors by Bayes' rule and so depends on</data><data key="conditions">When predicted probabilities are re-weighted by the ratio of new to old priors; miscalibrated scores give wrong corrections.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1943" source="mlsys.label-shift-estimation" target="mlsys.retraining"><data key="relation">alternative-to</data><data key="statement">is a label-free alternative, for prior changes, to</data><data key="conditions">When the shift is confined to class priors and fresh labels are unavailable or delayed.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1944" source="mlsys.slam" target="mlsys.map-representation"><data key="relation">produces</data><data key="statement">builds and maintains the</data><data key="conditions">When mapping runs online from the platform's own observations.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1945" source="mlsys.motion-planning" target="mlsys.map-representation"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">answers collision and clearance queries against the</data><data key="conditions">The representation must support the query the planner issues at planning rate, such as distance-to-obstacle or occupancy lookup.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1946" source="mlsys.on-device-memory-budget" target="mlsys.map-representation"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">bounds the resolution and extent of the</data><data key="conditions">When the map lives in onboard memory beside the perception and policy models.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1947" source="mlsys.motion-forecasting" target="mlsys.map-representation"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">conditions predicted trajectories on the lanes and free space in the</data><data key="conditions">When a prior or online map supplies road or room structure.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1948" source="mlsys.compute-cost-estimate" target="mlsys.training-compute-estimate"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">takes its operation count from</data><data key="conditions">When the job is dense-transformer training.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1949" source="mlsys.compute-cost-estimate" target="mlsys.model-flops-utilization"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">divides peak-rate hours by the expected</data><data key="conditions">When required operations are converted into wall-clock accelerator-hours.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1950" source="mlsys.compute-cost-estimate" target="mlsys.inference-compute-estimate"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">takes per-token work from</data><data key="conditions">When pricing a serving fleet rather than a training run.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1951" source="mlsys.dp-sgd" target="mlsys.time-to-train"><data key="relation">increases</data><data key="statement">adds per-example gradient computation and clipping to</data><data key="conditions">When per-example gradient and clipping overhead is not offset by fewer optimization steps or faster kernels at the same declared quality target.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1952" source="mlsys.retraining" target="mlsys.drift-detection"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">is triggered by</data><data key="conditions">When a detected shift is diagnosed as harmful rather than benign; drift alone is a signal to investigate, not proof of degraded quality.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1953" source="mlsys.fat-tree" target="mlsys.network-oversubscription"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">is commonly built, to save cost, with</data><data key="conditions">When a fat-tree is built with fewer spine ports than leaf downlinks to save cost.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1954" source="mlsys.dataset-shuffling" target="mlsys.experiment-reproducibility"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">breaks, unless its seed and shard order are recorded,</data><data key="conditions">When runs must visit examples in the same order to be compared.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1955" source="mlsys.supervised-fine-tuning" target="mlsys.chat-template"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">renders every example through</data><data key="conditions">For conversational or tool-using fine-tuning data turned into tokens before loss computation.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1956" source="mlsys.safety-integrity-level" target="mlsys.safety-certification"><data key="relation">determines</data><data key="statement">sets the rigor of evidence demanded by</data><data key="conditions">When the function's integrity level has been assigned by hazard analysis.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1957" source="mlsys.loop-stability-margin" target="mlsys.system-identification"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">takes its plant model from</data><data key="conditions">When an identified plant response is combined with a specified controller to compute the margin.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1958" source="mlsys.in-doubt-action" target="mlsys.durable-execution"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">blocks, until settled, the resumption of</data><data key="conditions">When a resumed workflow reaches a step whose effect may or may not have happened.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1959" source="mlsys.model-bandwidth-utilization" target="mlsys.utilization"><data key="relation">measures</data><data key="statement">reports, as an achieved fraction of aggregate peak bandwidth, the decode-phase</data><data key="conditions">When the phase is memory-bound, so model-required bytes per step rather than FLOPs set the ceiling; physical traffic beyond model-required bytes is excluded.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1960" source="mlsys.fairness-monitoring" target="mlsys.drift-detection"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">supplements group metrics with subgroup tests from</data><data key="conditions">When a subgroup's metric moves and the cause must be separated from model, label, or sampling changes.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1961" source="mlsys.tco" target="mlsys.compute-cost-estimate"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">allocates workload compute cost through</data><data key="conditions">When workload costs are allocated consistently with the ownership boundary and without double-counting capital, energy, or other included expenses.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1962" source="mlsys.agent-harness" target="mlsys.idempotency"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">retries tool steps safely through</data><data key="conditions">When a step is re-issued after a timeout or crash and the tool contract declares an idempotency key or deduplication.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1963" source="mlsys.wcet" target="mlsys.profiling"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">takes observed samples, as evidence but not as a bound, from</data><data key="conditions">When a measurement-based bound is argued from execution traces plus analysis of unobserved states.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1964" source="mlsys.interrupt-latency" target="mlsys.real-time-scheduling"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">limits the response-time budget available to</data><data key="conditions">An interrupt releases the scheduled task, and the response deadline is measured from the interrupt request; handler execution and task execution consume additional time.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1965" source="mlsys.precision-recall" target="mlsys.metrics"><data key="relation">specializes</data><data key="statement">is the two-error-type family of</data><data key="conditions">For classification or detection tasks where false positives and false negatives carry different costs.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1966" source="mlsys.decision-policy" target="mlsys.precision-recall"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">sets its operating threshold from the curve of</data><data key="conditions">When the policy thresholds a score and the two error rates must be traded explicitly.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1967" source="mlsys.instruction-hierarchy" target="mlsys.prompt-injection"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">lowers the success rate of</data><data key="conditions">When the model has been trained to respect source precedence or uses a provenance-marking prompt scheme validated against the applicable injection attacks, with trusted role assignments controlled outside attacker-supplied content.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1968" source="mlsys.instruction-hierarchy" target="mlsys.rlhf"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">learns instruction precedence using</data><data key="conditions">When human preference comparisons over instruction-following behavior train a reward model and reinforcement learning optimizes the policy against that reward.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1969" source="mlsys.restart-free-recovery" target="mlsys.training-goodput"><data key="relation">increases</data><data key="statement">raises, by avoiding full-job restart and reducing recovery losses, the</data><data key="conditions">When, under the same accelerator-time accounting, saved restart and rollback losses exceed replication, redundant-computation, reconfiguration, and reserved-capacity costs for failures within the supported failure model.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1970" source="mlsys.restart-free-recovery" target="mlsys.pipeline-parallel"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">replicates or re-partitions the stages of</data><data key="conditions">For pipeline-parallel jobs, where a failed stage's work can be absorbed by redundant stages or by re-partitioning survivors.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1971" source="mlsys.tensor-parallel" target="mlsys.memory-footprint"><data key="relation">reduces</data><data key="statement">shards a layer's parameters and activations to lower the per-device</data><data key="conditions">Aggregate footprint is unchanged; per-device savings depend on which tensors are partitioned.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1972" source="mlsys.sequence-parallel" target="mlsys.memory-footprint"><data key="relation">reduces</data><data key="statement">partitions activations along the sequence to lower the per-device</data><data key="conditions">Only activation memory shrinks; parameters and optimizer state are unaffected.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1973" source="mlsys.batch-size" target="mlsys.bound-regime"><data key="relation">determines</data><data key="statement">sets the arithmetic intensity, and so the binding ceiling, of weight-streaming kernels in</data><data key="conditions">Holds for kernels whose bytes are dominated by weights reused across the batch, such as GEMM and decode attention.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1974" source="mlsys.io-aware-attention" target="mlsys.locality"><data key="relation">increases</data><data key="statement">keeps attention tiles resident on chip to improve</data><data key="conditions">Requires on-chip storage large enough for the chosen tile and an exact tiled formulation.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1975" source="mlsys.mixed-precision" target="mlsys.bound-regime"><data key="relation">determines</data><data key="statement">lowers the bytes moved per operation for operands held in the reduced format, shifting a kernel's position in</data><data key="conditions">When the reduced format is applied to the operands that dominate traffic; master weights and accumulators keep full width, and attainable arithmetic throughput changes with the format as well.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1976" source="mlsys.lossless-fabric" target="mlsys.gray-failure"><data key="relation">causes</data><data key="statement">can propagate pause frames into stalls that look healthy to some observers, a form of</data><data key="conditions">Priority flow control enabled end to end; a single paused queue can block unrelated flows.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1977" source="mlsys.llm-request-scheduling" target="mlsys.ttft"><data key="relation">determines</data><data key="statement">sets, through its prefill-versus-decode priority, how long a new request waits for</data><data key="conditions">Continuous-batching engines where prefill and decode share iterations.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1978" source="mlsys.cache-aware-routing" target="mlsys.ttft"><data key="relation">reduces</data><data key="statement">skips recomputing a cached prefix and so lowers</data><data key="conditions">Requests share prefixes and the routed replica still holds the cached state.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1979" source="mlsys.llm-request-scheduling" target="mlsys.kv-cache"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">preempts in-flight requests by evicting or swapping blocks, bounding resident</data><data key="conditions">Continuous-batching engines with preemption; recompute-on-resume costs prefill time.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1980" source="mlsys.distributed-tracing" target="mlsys.tail-latency"><data key="relation">measures</data><data key="statement">attributes a slow request's time to the spans behind</data><data key="conditions">Request-scoped tracing enabled across every hop on the path, with sampling that keeps slow requests.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1981" source="mlsys.graceful-degradation" target="mlsys.metastable-failure"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">sheds work to a cheaper mode that breaks the sustaining loop of</data><data key="conditions">The degraded mode must cost less than the overloaded path and be entered before saturation.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1982" source="mlsys.sliced-evaluation" target="mlsys.distribution-shift"><data key="relation">detects</data><data key="statement">localizes, by a drop concentrated in one slice, the population affected by</data><data key="conditions">Slices are defined on production inputs and labels or proxies exist for the window.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1983" source="mlsys.off-policy-evaluation" target="mlsys.proxy-objective"><data key="relation">detects</data><data key="statement">exposes divergence between the optimized proxy and the estimated deployed outcome of</data><data key="conditions">When logged propensities are known, the logging and candidate policies overlap, and the intended outcome is observed separately from the proxy.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1984" source="mlsys.cost-sensitive-decision" target="mlsys.proxy-objective"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">re-weights decisions by the real cost of each error type, correcting a misaligned</data><data key="conditions">Error costs must be stated per outcome, not inferred from the proxy.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1985" source="mlsys.thermal-dynamics" target="mlsys.measurement-variability"><data key="relation">causes</data><data key="statement">makes short runs overstate sustained performance, a source of</data><data key="conditions">Measurement windows shorter than the device's thermal time constant.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1986" source="mlsys.quantization" target="mlsys.energy-per-inference"><data key="relation">reduces</data><data key="statement">cuts bytes moved and energy per multiply-accumulate, lowering</data><data key="conditions">The target hardware has low-precision datapaths; dequantization overhead must not dominate.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1987" source="mlsys.prefix-cache" target="mlsys.cost-per-token"><data key="relation">reduces</data><data key="statement">avoids recomputing shared prefixes, lowering</data><data key="conditions">Requests share long prefixes such as system prompts; cache hits must be routed to the holding replica.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1988" source="mlsys.llm-call-tracing" target="mlsys.over-refusal"><data key="relation">detects</data><data key="statement">attributes a refusal to the input filter, the model, or the output filter, localizing</data><data key="conditions">Filter decisions are recorded as spans alongside the model call, and the refused requests are labeled benign by an independent rubric.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1989" source="mlsys.supervised-fine-tuning" target="mlsys.over-refusal"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">trains compliance on curated borderline-benign requests, reducing</data><data key="conditions">The curated set must be reviewed so it does not also teach compliance on harmful requests.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1990" source="mlsys.agent-interruption" target="mlsys.model-directed-control-flow"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">lets an operator override the next step chosen by</data><data key="conditions">Interruption boundaries must sit at tool-call granularity so a pending action is not half-applied.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1991" source="mlsys.egress-control" target="mlsys.prompt-injection"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">removes the exfiltration channel exploited by</data><data key="conditions">Allow-listed destinations; injected instructions can still act within the sandbox.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1992" source="mlsys.checkpoint-io" target="mlsys.storage-tiering"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">stages snapshots on a fast local tier before draining them through</data><data key="conditions">The local tier must hold several snapshots and drain faster than the checkpoint interval.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1993" source="mlsys.llm-call-tracing" target="mlsys.context-staleness"><data key="relation">measures</data><data key="statement">records the passages and index timestamps needed to judge</data><data key="conditions">When recorded index timestamps are compared with source versions or validity intervals; recency alone does not establish freshness.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1994" source="mlsys.watchdog-timer" target="mlsys.feedback-delay"><data key="relation">detects</data><data key="statement">reports a missed refresh deadline as a fault, revealing excess</data><data key="conditions">The window is derived from the control period and the refresh is placed after actuation.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1995" source="mlsys.mixed-criticality" target="mlsys.priority-inversion"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">partitions time and resources so lower-criticality tasks cannot hold what high-criticality tasks need, preventing</data><data key="conditions">Shared resources must have bounded access or be partitioned; priority inheritance still applies inside a partition.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1996" source="mlsys.cross-stack-profiling" target="mlsys.input-pipeline"><data key="relation">measures</data><data key="statement">attributes device idle gaps to the host-side stages of</data><data key="conditions">Host and device timelines are correlated on one clock.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1997" source="mlsys.evaluation-harness" target="mlsys.catastrophic-forgetting"><data key="relation">detects</data><data key="statement">reruns a fixed base-capability suite on each checkpoint, revealing</data><data key="conditions">The suite and prompts are held fixed across checkpoints.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1998" source="mlsys.regularization" target="mlsys.catastrophic-forgetting"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">penalizes drift from the base parameters or outputs, limiting</data><data key="conditions">The penalty, such as a KL term to the base model, must be weighted against the target-task loss.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e1999" source="mlsys.peft" target="mlsys.catastrophic-forgetting"><data key="relation">mitigates</data><data key="statement">freezes the base weights so adaptation cannot overwrite them, limiting</data><data key="conditions">Holds when the adapter capacity is small relative to the base and base weights stay frozen.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e2000" source="mlsys.release-gate" target="mlsys.model-feature-compatibility"><data key="relation">detects</data><data key="statement">checks the candidate's tokenizer, schema, and operator set against the serving stack, revealing broken</data><data key="conditions">The gate runs against the same runtime image that will serve the model.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e2001" source="mlsys.backfill-scheduling" target="mlsys.gang-scheduling"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">uses whole-job allocation reservations established by</data><data key="conditions">When the backfill planner consults reserved start times and resource sets for gang-allocated jobs and admits other jobs only when their bounded resource use preserves those starts.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e2002" source="mlsys.agent-memory" target="mlsys.prompt-injection"><data key="relation">enables</data><data key="statement">enables persistent</data><data key="conditions">When attacker-controlled instructions can be stored in agent memory and later retrieved into a context where the model may treat them as authoritative.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e2003" source="mlsys.tensor-parallel" target="mlsys.tpot"><data key="relation">reduces</data><data key="statement">pools the memory bandwidth of several devices to shorten</data><data key="conditions">For the same model, precision, batch, and output workload, when tensor sharding reduces per-device decode memory traffic and the saved memory-access time exceeds the added collective, synchronization, and execution overhead.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e2004" source="mlsys.service-level-agreement" target="mlsys.slo"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">constrains the acceptable targets for</data><data key="conditions">When the internal objective covers the same service indicator, population, and measurement window as the contractual commitment and must provide a stricter target with room for intervention.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e2005" source="mlsys.compute-cost-estimate" target="mlsys.cost-per-token"><data key="relation">produces</data><data key="statement">produces an estimate of</data><data key="conditions">When estimated accelerator expenditure is divided by a declared processed-token or generated-token count and the reported cost boundary covers accelerator compute only.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e2006" source="mlsys.deterministic-field-network" target="mlsys.timing-jitter"><data key="relation">limits</data><data key="statement">bounds the transport contribution to</data><data key="conditions">When control messages use synchronized, fixed transmission slots with bounded interfering traffic; the bound concerns network-induced variation rather than all sensing, computation, and actuation jitter.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e2007" source="mlsys.self-supervised-learning" target="mlsys.contrastive-learning"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">often trains through</data><data key="conditions">When positives and negatives are constructed from the data itself, without example-level labels.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e2008" source="mlsys.data-parallel" target="mlsys.state-sharding"><data key="relation">uses</data><data key="statement">partitions state across workers through</data><data key="conditions">When each worker processes different examples but holds only a shard of parameters, gradients, or optimizer state.</data></edge>
    <edge id="e2009" source="mlsys.shared-memory-bank-conflict" target="mlsys.memory-bandwidth"><data key="relation">reduces</data><data key="statement">reduces effective shared-memory</data><data key="conditions">Different addresses in the same shared-memory bank require serialized requests; same-address broadcast is excluded.</data></edge>
  </graph>
</graphml>
